## Bundled components (shipped in the Windows installer)

### Hamlib (rigctld) — <https://hamlib.github.io/>
Hamlib **4.7.1**, bundled as a runtime on **every** shipped platform: Windows,
Linux (.deb, AppImage, and both Raspberry Pi bases) and macOS.

License, checked **per file** rather than taken from the repository-level claim
(2026-08-24): the Hamlib **library** is **LGPL-2.1-or-later** and the
**tools** — `rigctld`, `rigctl`, `rotctld`, `rotctl`, which are what Nexus
launches — are **GPL-2.0-or-later**. Verified two independent ways: upstream
SPDX headers in the 4.7.1 source (5 × `GPL-2.0-or-later`, 14 ×
`LGPL-2.1-or-later`, and **zero** `GPL-2.0-only` files), and Debian's own audit
of the same package (`License: LGPL-2.1+ and GPL-2+`). Both are *or-later*, so
both may be used under GPL-3, which is what Nexus is (GPL-3.0-only). Had any
part been GPL-2-**only** it could not have shipped here at all.

Nexus does not link Hamlib. It launches `rigctld` as a **separate process** and
speaks the rigctld TCP protocol to it; `rigctl` backs the baud-ladder probe and
the model list, and `rotctld` drives rotators.

Staging is reproducible and the binaries are **not committed to this
repository**: `scripts/fetch-hamlib.sh` unpacks the official Windows release
(SHA-256 pinned) and `scripts/fetch-hamlib-unix.sh` builds Linux/macOS from the
official source tarball (SHA-256 pinned). Both place the binaries and Hamlib's
own license texts under `src-tauri/resources/hamlib/`, which the Tauri bundle
includes (`tauri.conf.json` → `bundle.resources`). The license files travel with
every installer; refer to them and to the Hamlib project for exact terms.

Before 2026-08-24 only Windows carried the bundle. Linux relied on the .deb's
`libhamlib-utils` dependency — which left **AppImage** users, who install
nothing by design, with no CAT until they found out unaided that they needed a
Hamlib package; macOS relied on `brew install hamlib` the same way.

