## Rig-control protocol interoperability (no vendor code bundled)

Nexus controls transceivers over their manufacturers' **published** control
protocols. Each implementation is **independent, original code** written from the
public protocol documentation to interoperate with hardware the operator owns — a
long-standing, widely-practiced form of interoperability (Hamlib, WSJT-X, N1MM, and
many others control the same radios the same way). **No manufacturer SDK, library,
or source code is copied or bundled.**

### Icom CI-V
The native Icom CI-V transport (`crates/tempo-audio/src/civ/`) is an original
implementation of the CI-V command set (frequency, mode, PTT, levels/meters, and the
`0x27` scope-waveform stream), written from **Icom's published CI-V reference** (the
command tables in Icom's operating manuals — e.g. the IC-7300 §19 command list).
Command opcodes and byte layouts are functional facts of the wire protocol; no Icom
software or manual text is reproduced.

### FlexRadio SmartSDR
The FlexRadio panadapter path (`crates/tempo-net/src/flex{cat,vita,disc}.rs` and
`crates/tempo-audio/src/flexspectrum.rs`) is an original implementation of a SmartSDR
Ethernet-API client, written from FlexRadio's **publicly-documented API**
(<https://github.com/flexradio/smartsdr-api-docs>) and the **open VITA-49 Radio
Transport standard** (VITA), and validated against synthetic frames. The FFT-packet
field layout is cross-checked against the open-source **FlexLib** reference as a wire-
format fact, not a code copy. **FlexRadio's proprietary FlexLib .NET library is NOT
included or redistributed** — it is redistributable with third-party apps and requires
a license only for *commercial* products, and Nexus neither bundles it nor is a
commercial product. FlexRadio publishes this API expressly for third-party developers,
and an ecosystem of independent open-source clients exists (e.g. K3TZR's xLib6000,
flexlib-go, and the GPLv3 AetherSDR).

### Trademarks
**Icom** and IC-model names, and **FlexRadio**, **SmartSDR**, and FLEX-model names,
are trademarks of Icom Inc. and FlexRadio Systems respectively. Nexus uses them only
**nominatively** — to state which radios it is compatible with. Nexus is an
independent project and is **not affiliated with, sponsored by, or endorsed by** Icom
or FlexRadio.

