## Redistributed SatNOGS material

SatNOGS data (**CC BY-SA 4.0**, <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/>,
© the SatNOGS / Libre Space Foundation community) is redistributed by this
project in four places. None is compiled into a binary; the licence and its
attribution travel inside each artifact so that they survive it being copied
out of this tree. Creative Commons designates GPL-3.0 a **BY-SA Compatible
License**, so adapted BY-SA 4.0 material may be redistributed under GPLv3 with
attribution preserved.

### 1. The amateur satellite catalog + TLE mirror (published, downloaded by every install)

`scripts/gen-tles.mjs` runs in CI every six hours and publishes `tles.json` /
`tles.txt` as a GitHub Release asset that <https://hamradiotools.io/nexus/> 302s
to, and that every Nexus install refreshes from and caches to disk. Its content
is derived from:

- the **SatNOGS DB** (<https://db.satnogs.org>) — the satellite population,
  names, statuses and transmitter records, **CC BY-SA 4.0**;
- the **SatNOGS TLE API** — orbital elements, same terms; and
- **CelesTrak** (<https://celestrak.org>, Dr. T.S. Kelso) — orbital elements
  from the `amateur` and `satnogs` GP groups, used under CelesTrak's terms.

Because the population and status data are BY-SA, the published catalog is an
adaptation of BY-SA material and is offered under **CC BY-SA 4.0**. The
manifest carries that statement in its own `attribution` field, and the
Release notes carry it too. Our thanks to the SatNOGS community, whose
curation is what makes a list of *amateur* satellites possible at all — no
element source alone can say which birds carry a working amateur transmitter.

### 2. The bundled seed snapshot (committed here, shipped inside the installer)

`src-tauri/resources/tles/tles.json` is that same manifest, committed into this
tree and bundled into every installer as an app resource (`resources/tles/`).
It is there so a fresh install has the amateur satellite catalog on first
launch — complete, and with no network — instead of a near-empty list until its
first successful fetch; the app then refreshes from the published mirror
exactly as it always has. `scripts/release-prep` re-runs the generator and
re-cuts this file at release time, so a shipped snapshot is as current as the
release that carries it.

Being the same payload, it is an adaptation of the same three sources:

- the **SatNOGS DB** (<https://db.satnogs.org>) — the satellite population,
  names, statuses and transmitter records, **CC BY-SA 4.0**;
- the **SatNOGS TLE API** — orbital elements, same terms; and
- **CelesTrak** (<https://celestrak.org>, Dr. T.S. Kelso) — orbital elements
  from the `amateur` and `satnogs` GP groups, used under CelesTrak's terms.

Of the four, this is the copy that reaches an operator most directly: every
installer carries one. It is therefore the clearest case for this section's
rule — it is **not** compiled into the executable, and that is deliberate. It
ships as a standalone JSON file carrying its own `attribution` object, written
by `scripts/gen-tles.mjs`: `"license": "CC-BY-SA-4.0"` plus a `"text"` that
credits the SatNOGS DB and its contributors, CelesTrak and Dr. T.S. Kelso, and
the SatNOGS TLE API, and states that the derived catalog is offered under
CC BY-SA 4.0. That field is present in the committed file and in every
regeneration of it, so the licence and the credit travel with the data if the
file is ever copied out of the installer. The adapted catalog is offered under
**CC BY-SA 4.0**, as above.

